0.1.1
- update_refseq and update_genbank are replaced by update_genomes, which updates both databases in one run from one mirror, one pair of genome_dirs files and into one release directory (--ftp_directory, --new_directory, --ftp_genome_dirs_file, --old_genome_dirs_file). RefSeqManager and GenBankManager are folded into GenomeManager, told apart by the accession prefix; the reports are report_gcf.log/gcf_to_review.log and report_gca.log/gca_to_review.log. The GenBank selection that lived in GenBankManager (gca_selection.log, --new_refseq_genome_dirs_file, --arc/--bac_assembly_summary) is gone: select_genomes makes that decision
- update_refseq and update_genbank now decide a genome held by both the previous release and the mirror from the MD5 of its genomic FASTA as recorded in the two md5checksums.txt files, in place of copying both directories to temporary space, decompressing and checksumming every file. The mirror's directory is taken whole, md5checksums.txt included; if the FASTA is unchanged the directories named by config.GTDB_DERIVED_DIRS_TO_COPY (prodigal, rna_silva_<version>, trna, rna_ltp_<version>) are copied across from the previous release, otherwise the derived data is left to be regenerated. The report now says "genomic FASTA file unchanged" or "genomic FASTA file changed" per genome, replacing the incomplete/modified/unmodified/new_metadata/new_hashes/old_folder_dir vocabulary; a derived directory the previous release lacks is noted in the review report. A dry run makes the comparison and writes the same reports as a real run, and copies nothing
- added SILVA_VERSION, LTP_VERSION and GTDB_DERIVED_DIRS_TO_COPY to config.py
- update_refseq determines the genomes to remove, add and compare from the accessions in the FTP and previous release genome_dirs files alone, and no longer takes --arc_assembly_summary/--bac_assembly_summary: the mirror is built from the selection, so every RefSeq genome on it belongs in the release. Its report_gcf.log rows carry UNDEFINED in the domain column, the summary files having been the only source of a domain
- ncbi_genome_sync now takes --gtdb_selected_genomes, the table select_genomes writes, and treats it as the definition of the mirror: every genome directory under --root the table does not list is removed before anything is fetched, each recorded in <base>.rm before it is deleted. --retry syncs a .fail/.bad file without removing anything, and --dry-run reports how many directories would be removed, added and checked for updates, listing the removals in <base>.rm_dry_run without touching the mirror
- ncbi_genome_sync removes genomes in parallel instead of serially, and its climb up the emptied triplet directories asks rmdir rather than listing first: the pass is nothing but NFS round trips, and that is four fewer per genome as well as overlapping the rest. Measured on the production NFS server: 113 genomes/s serial, 465 at the default 8 threads
- ncbi_genome_sync takes --nfs-jobs (default 8), sizing the mirror walk and the removal; these touch the local mirror rather than NCBI, so --rate does not bound them
- removed clean_ftp; pruning the mirror is now the first step of ncbi_genome_sync, which holds the selection that says what the mirror should contain
- ncbi_genome_sync --verify/--verify-only, given the selection, now verify both halves of "the mirror equals the selection": every listed genome present and md5 clean, and nothing else present. Directories the selection does not list go to <base>.extra and fail the verification; --delete removes them
- ncbi_genome_sync interface: --root is now required with no default, --rate defaults to 20, and -l/--log is defined by the sync itself with .fail/.bad/.rm/.extra written beside it
- ncbi_genome_sync orchestration moved into an NCBIGenomeSync class with a run() method, so main.py drives it like every other manager; the thread workers and the state they share stay module-level
- list_genomes now takes -g/--gtdb_selected_genomes instead of -n/--new_list_genomes, and writes a genome_dirs file holding only the genomes of that release; its '-missing' and '-extra' reports are gone, that check being ncbi_genome_sync --verify. --genome_dir moved from -g to -d
- select_genomes ignores genomes NCBI serves no directory for (ftp_path na) in RefSeq and GenBank alike. Because RefSeq covers a genome only when the RefSeq assembly was itself selected, an unserved RefSeq genome no longer suppresses its GenBank counterpart: that copy is selected in its place, with the notes column naming the RefSeq assembly it stands in for and why. 639 RefSeq bacterial genomes are unserved on r237, and each would otherwise have left the release entirely
0.1.0
- moved to stream lined workflow where genomes for the next GTDB release are selected first before syncing with NCBI genome data
- added ncbi_metadata_sync, which populates the root NCBI directory of a release: the four per-domain assembly summary files (gzipped), and taxonomy/ holding the md5 verified NCBI taxonomy alongside standardised_taxonomy/ with the 7 rank taxonomy for the given release number
- assembly summary files may now be gzipped; every reader goes through ncbi_utils.open_summary, which takes either form
- removed parse_ncbi_taxonomy as a subcommand; the 7 rank taxonomy is generated by ncbi_metadata_sync
- the NCBI taxonomy parser no longer writes a line per assembly with an unresolvable taxid (2,521 of them on r237); it reports the count with three examples
- the NCBI taxonomy parser now reports through the 'timestamp' logger, so its output reaches the command's log file rather than only the console
- added select_genomes, which determines the genomes comprising a new release from the NCBI assembly summary files alone
- select_genomes now writes version_status and excluded_from_refseq, so its table is directly the input to ncbi_genome_sync; without version_status the sync fetches assembly_status.txt over HTTP for every genome
- fixed count_lines reading a gzipped file as text, which made select_genomes fail on the compressed summaries ncbi_metadata_sync writes
- ncbi_genome_sync now strips .gz when naming its .log/.fail/.bad outputs, so syncing from gtdb_selected_genomes.tsv.gz no longer yields gtdb_selected_genomes.tsv.gz.fail
- fixed ncbi_utils rejecting the '# assembly_accession' header NCBI wrote until 2020, which blocked ncbi_sync, update_refseq and update_genbank on archived summary files
- fixed parse_ncbi_taxonomy writing failed_filters.tsv to the working directory; it is now written beside the taxonomy it describes
- renamed ftp_manager.py to ncbi_ftp_manager.py and ftp_manager_tools.py to ncbi_ftp_manager_tools.py; GenericDatabaseManager is now GenericManager
- renamed the ncbi_sync command and ncbi_sync.py to ncbi_genome_sync; the mirror lock file keeps its .ncbi_sync.lock name so an upgrade cannot let two syncs share a root
